WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.245
Lesson 2.7.

00:00:02.245 --> 00:00:05.360
Advanced AMM design and LVR.

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.208
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:11.210
able to know the major AMM designs and

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:15.905
measure an LP's real cost with loss-versus-rebalancing (LVR).

00:00:17.205 --> 00:00:18.495
Here's the plan.

00:00:18.495 --> 00:00:20.180
First, the key ideas.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:22.895
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:22.895 --> 00:00:24.420
Then, your checklist.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.582
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:27.882 --> 00:00:31.750
First, Constant product (x·y=k): works for any

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:35.507
pair; spreads liquidity across all prices.

00:00:35.507 --> 00:00:39.570
Next, StableSwap: for assets that should

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:43.636
trade near 1:1 (stablecoins, LST/ETH).

00:00:43.636 --> 00:00:46.741
Blends constant-sum (flat, low slippage near the

00:00:46.741 --> 00:00:49.910
peg) with constant-product (safety away from it),

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:52.491
tuned by an amplification parameter.

00:00:52.491 --> 00:00:58.435
Then, Weighted pools: more than two tokens or uneven

00:00:58.435 --> 00:01:04.231
weights (e.g. 80/20), with invariant ∏ xᵢ^wᵢ = k.

00:01:04.231 --> 00:01:07.212
An 80/20 pool has less impermanent

00:01:07.212 --> 00:01:09.386
loss on the 80% token.

00:01:09.386 --> 00:01:13.616
Also, Concentrated liquidity: positions in price ranges

00:01:13.616 --> 00:01:17.316
("ticks"); the pool tracks √price internally.

00:01:17.316 --> 00:01:21.337
On top of that, Hooks and dynamic fees: newer

00:01:21.337 --> 00:01:25.970
designs (e.g. Uniswap v4, 2025) let pools run custom

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:31.078
code at swap time: dynamic fees, limit orders, oracles.

00:01:31.078 --> 00:01:33.158
Each hook is extra contract risk.

00:01:34.458 --> 00:01:40.356
And finally, LVR (loss-versus-rebalancing): pool prices only update when

00:01:40.356 --> 00:01:45.423
arbitrageurs trade against LPs after prices move elsewhere.

00:01:45.423 --> 00:01:49.201
LVR measures what LPs lose to that arbitrage,

00:01:49.201 --> 00:01:53.772
versus a portfolio that rebalances at market prices.

00:01:53.772 --> 00:01:58.850
For a full-range constant-product pool, LVR ≈ σ²/8 of

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:03.287
pool value per year (σ = annual volatility).

00:02:03.287 --> 00:02:06.962
Fees must beat LVR, not just impermanent loss.

00:02:08.262 --> 00:02:12.717
Let's look at the chart: Loss-versus-rebalancing vs volatility.

00:02:12.717 --> 00:02:14.277
Take a moment with it.

00:02:14.277 --> 00:02:16.687
It's everything we've just said, in one picture.

00:02:17.987 --> 00:02:20.502
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:02:20.502 --> 00:02:26.407
An ETH/USDC full-range pool with ETH volatility

00:02:26.407 --> 00:02:31.997
80%/yr: the calculator gives LVR ≈ 8.0%/yr.

00:02:31.997 --> 00:02:34.841
With 12% fee APR, LPs

00:02:34.841 --> 00:02:38.373
keep ≈ +4%/yr before gas.

00:02:38.373 --> 00:02:42.950
If volatility rises to 110%, LVR ≈ 15%/yr,

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.763
and the same fees now lose money.

00:02:46.763 --> 00:02:49.820
Higher-volatility pairs need much higher fee tiers.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.580
Here's your checklist.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:54.115
Do it now, for real.

00:02:54.115 --> 00:02:56.654
First, I choose pool type by pair:

00:02:56.654 --> 00:03:00.653
StableSwap for pegged pairs, weighted/CL otherwise.

00:03:00.653 --> 00:03:03.272
Next, I compare fee APR

00:03:03.272 --> 00:03:05.973
with LVR, not just IL.

00:03:05.973 --> 00:03:09.521
And finally, Hook contracts reviewed like any other contract.

00:03:10.821 --> 00:03:12.031
Question 1.

00:03:12.031 --> 00:03:14.726
Why do stable pairs use StableSwap curves?

00:03:19.146 --> 00:03:21.351
The answer: They give very low slippage

00:03:21.351 --> 00:03:23.446
near the peg, where these pairs trade.

00:03:24.746 --> 00:03:25.991
Question 2.

00:03:25.991 --> 00:03:27.626
What does LVR measure?

00:03:32.046 --> 00:03:35.308
The answer: What LPs lose to arbitrageurs

00:03:35.308 --> 00:03:38.026
because pool prices lag the market.

00:03:39.326 --> 00:03:40.596
Question 3.

00:03:40.596 --> 00:03:45.741
Volatility 60%/yr: approximate LVR for a full-range pool?

00:03:50.161 --> 00:03:53.438
The answer: 0.6²/8 = 4.5%

00:03:53.438 --> 00:03:56.336
of pool value per year.

00:03:57.636 --> 00:03:58.881
Let's recap.

00:03:58.881 --> 00:04:03.311
Constant product (x·y=k): works for any pair.

00:04:03.311 --> 00:04:06.847
StableSwap: for assets that should

00:04:06.847 --> 00:04:10.986
trade near 1:1 (stablecoins, LST/ETH).

00:04:10.986 --> 00:04:17.199
Weighted pools: more than two tokens or uneven weights

00:04:17.199 --> 00:04:22.131
(e.g. 80/20), with invariant ∏ xᵢ^wᵢ = k.

00:04:22.131 --> 00:04:26.031
Concentrated liquidity: positions in price ranges ("ticks").

00:04:27.331 --> 00:04:29.846
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:04:29.846 --> 00:04:33.911
Next up, Lesson 2.8: The MEV supply chain.
